Description of the Program:

myshell is a command line shell written in c. The shell is created to operate in Unix/Linux environments and supports a suite of 8 internal commands. myshell also has the ability support unknown commands using fork() and execvp() to duplicate the process and execute using the environment. 

Algorithm:

MYSHELL(length_A, A) // Where A is the input argument array for the running program

	maxNumCommands = 10 // Variable is a constant          

	Set environment variable "shell" to absolute path of the shell

	for n commands input by the user:
		
		cwd = current working directory
		print "cwd>"
		input = GET_STRING_INPUT(input)
		commands = new array from 0 to maxNumCommands
		commands = PARSE_INPUT(input, commands)
	
		command = commands[0]

		if command is NULL or Empty:
			ignore and continue

		if command == "mycd":
			if commands has > 2 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				CD(commands[1])
			end if else

		else if command == "myclr":
			if commands has > 1 argument:
				print Error
			else:
				Call the system to clear the screen using "clear"
			end if else

		else if command == "mydir":
			if commands has > 2 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				DIR(commands[1])
			end if else

		else if command == "myenviron":
			if commands has > 1 argument:
				print Error
			else:
				PRINT_ENVIRONMENT_STRINGS()
			end if else

		else if command == "myecho:
			if commands has < 2 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				comment = new array, contains commands[1] to commands[maxNumCommands - 1]
				ECHO(comment)
			end if else
	
		else if command == "myhelp"
			if commands has > 1 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				Call the system to print readme with more tag using "more readme"
			end if else

		else if command == "mypause":
			if commands has > 1 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				PAUSE()
			end if else

		else if command == "myquit":
			if commands has > 1 arguments:
				print Error
			else:
				EXIT(0)
			end if else

		else:
			// external commands
			fork into child process
			
			child:
				Set environment variable "parent" to "shell"
				Use execvp to execute commands
				EXIT(0)
			parent:
				wait till child finishes

		end if else

	end loop

end function

GET_USER_INPUT(A) // Where A is the string in which input is being stored
	B = user input
	
	for i = 0 to length of B:
		if B[i] == '\r' OR B[i] == '\n':
			B[i] = '\0'
			end loop
	end loop

end function

PARSE_INPUT(A, B) // Where A is the string input and B is the array parsed input is stored in
	
	delimeter = ' '
	counter = 0
	token = the first word in input separated by del

	while token is not NULL:
		commands[counter] = token
		token = next word in input separated by del

	end loop

end function
	
PAUSE():
	print pause prompt
	wait for user to press enter

end function

ECHO(input): // Where input is the string to echo

	for each word in input
		print "word "
	end loop

	print '\n'

end function

CD(dir) // Where dir is the string that contains the directory
	if directory is NULL OR Empty:
		cwd = current working directory
		print cwd
	else:
		change directory to dir
		new_cwd = current working directory
		set environment variable "PWD" to new_cwd
	end if else

end function

DIR(dir) // Where dir is the string that contains the directory

	if dir is NULL OR Empty:
		Call system to execute "ls"
	else: 
		Call system to execute "ls dir"
	end if else

end function

Algorithm Analysis:

Starting with analysis of the functions used withing myshell, we get:

	DIR - O(1)
	CD - O(1)
	ECHO - O(n) - The number of loops is dependent on the length n of the <comment> string
	PAUSE - O(1)
	PARSE_INPUT - O(1) - The max number of tokens will always be 10 so the function runs on constant time
	GET_USER_INPUT - O(n) - the number of loops is dependent on the length n of the input string

MYSHELL would then run with by the following:

	the while loop is runs n times dependent on the number of times the user wishes to input commands. Each child will run on constant time not accounting for what the OS has to do to execute external commands. We get

	O(n)(O(1) + O(1) + O(n) + O(1) + O(1) + O(n) + O(n))

	giving a runtime of O(n^2) for MYSHELL.

Description of Operations and Commands:

The 8 built in commands supported are as follows:

	mycd <directory> - changes the current active directory. If <directory> is left empty, 
	                   the current directory is displayed. getcwd() is used to get the
			   the current directory if no directory is given. If a directory is
			   given, chdir() is used to change the directory and setenv() is used
			   to update the "PWD" environment variable that keeps track of the
			   current directory.

	myclr - clears the screen. Uses system() and passes through the "clear" command to request
		the Unix/Linux terminal clear the screen.

	mydir <directory> - list the contents of <directory>. If <directory> is empty list contents 
			    of current directory. Uses snprintf to build the command based on whether
			    or not a directory was added as an argument. If there was no argument,	
			    the command sent to system() is ls to display the current directories
			    files. If there is an argument the command sent to system() is
			    ls <directory> to print our the files in <directory>

	myenviron - list all environment variables. Uses the external environment variable to print
		    all environment variables using a for loop.

	myecho <comment> - echoes <comment> back to the screen. Uses a for loop to print out the
			   <comment> given as an argument.

	myhelp - displays readme with the more tag. Uses system() with the command "more readme"

	mypause - pauses the shell until the "enter" key is pressed. Uses getchar() to wait for the
		  user to press the enter key before continuing normal shell operations.

	myquit - exits shell using exit(0).

The shell is used by entering one of the previous commands or an external command into the prompt. The command is then executed for the user. The shell will not execute any external cd commands to be processed within the child process.

Performance Description:

	Time:
	Using the Unix/Linux built in time command:
		real    2m36.207s
		user    0m0.014s
		sys     0m0.025s

	Was the measured time running for both internal and external commands. The total
	time spent running between the kernel and the program was 0.039s. The real elapsed
	time is much longer due to awaiting input from the user. 

	Using only internal commands, the measured time is as follows:
		real    1m45.602s
		user    0m0.018s
		sys     0m0.008s
	
	The total time spent executing commands is 0.026s. The real elapsed time is again
	much longer due to awaiting the users input.

	Using only external commands (at least 8):
		real    0m42.204s
		user    0m0.006s
		sys     0m0.026s

	The total time spent executing external commands in the kernel is 0.026s. The
	0.006s spent executing commands in the program was to quit the program using 
	"myquit" and EXIT(). The real elapsed time is again much longer due to awaiting the users
	input.

	Memory:
	Using valgrind to monitor memory usage, code ==19269== was reported after execution 
	of the parent process and after the execution of each child process. This code
	gives that the program run was clean with no memory errors or detected leaks.

	Other Descriptors:
	The maximum length for any command is 10, this means that the max number of words 	for the "myecho" command is 9.

	The maximum character length for each string in a list of commands and arguments is
	100. Meaning that each command and argument must be less than 100 characters long.

	The maximum directory length is 1024. Every time a directory is retrieved it must
	be within the 1024 character limit.

Description of Environment Concepts:

myshell uses environment variables to trace and manage its processes. It stores the absolute path into the "shell" variable which is both the master shell and parent shell path. When a child is forked, the absolute path of the parent is passed and stored into the "parent" variable for child to parent memory before it is replaced by the path of created by execvp. On the other hand, PWD ensures that any change in directory is remembered in the system and every process knows what the current directory is.

Overall Layout and Display of Understanding:

The shell is designed to loop until the user quits the shell. The maximum command length is 100, the max number of commands is 10, and the max directory size is 1024. The method used to accomplish this is a while(1) loop. The current directory is fetched at the beginning of every loop to be displayed in the command line prompt. User input is read and parsed using functions that utilize fgets(), strcspn()-for sanitization of the string, and strtok. All commands are modeled using if-else statements. Most commands that could not be executed in a simple system call are handled within their own functions whose names are given based on the command. All internal commands execute withing the context of the shell, external commands are handled by child processes. The parent will fork() a child process and wait() for a child process to complete before continuing. The child process sets the "parent" variable to the absolute path of the parent process for process memory and then executes the external command using execvp.

Using fork() for the shell to handle external commands without crashing due to errors the shell itself cannot handle. If execvp is unable to properly execute a command, the child is left to handle the problem and the parent stay unaffected even if the child crashes. Using wait() insures that the system stays synchronized and the user cannot continue to use the parent process while the child is running. Environment variables help ensures the state of the process is not forgotten when an external command is run. 


